#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176058 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175704 :question/id #uuid "0b146642-914c-52ce-9939-86752e95dcd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11013" :question/problem "If $\\tan x = -\\dfrac{2}{3},$ find the value of $\\tan (-x+7\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan{x}.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\tan{x}$ is $\\pi.$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\tan{x}$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\tan(-x) = -\\tan{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\tan{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x+7\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x+\\pi \\cdot 7) \\\\  & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\tan{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{2}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\tan (-x+7\\pi)=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66a1733a-d7bb-5c86-a154-9e54422053a7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176347 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19df7d47-b87b-5e46-8360-7533c525ec2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266358} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2908b8-2ead-5a77-be9f-804bc07bb7fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266672} {:answer/id #uuid "6ccc1b1b-9a04-5bb8-a591-9f5cc4c7efc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267102} {:answer/id #uuid "8b14d04b-7661-55d9-a6d2-74ec1e93a05a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267392} {:answer/id #uuid "a5bfc5ef-972c-5d0d-9011-6abdfdf11b45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19df7d47-b87b-5e46-8360-7533c525ec2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266358} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6737d96-a059-592e-9cfb-df4b5f79acd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268703}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ccd7ae50-1c4e-5bcc-8b8b-d405f5154f0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-136548" :question/problem "If $tan⁡a=-2,$ then $tan⁡(-a)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-a) & =-tan⁡a \\\\  & =-(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176348 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7302b1a-f67e-5531-ba08-f2741b2ee230" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2} + 12π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174347} {:answer/id #uuid "fc787e13-420f-50ab-936b-a893f957c41d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2} + 12π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174348} {:answer/id #uuid "1981b1cd-854c-58f3-8334-54b406265222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174349} {:answer/id #uuid "32fdf81a-926d-5419-8f2b-8c784eb0e3ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174350} {:answer/id #uuid "7a2a383c-1b09-59ee-a452-9b72e747e3a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174351}] :answer-field/id #uui