order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-a) & =-tan⁡a \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "342f578b-d80d-5790-a353-a30b6906380a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272852} {:answer/id #uuid "4f68c603-8ad9-5107-bdbf-c0638fd5673a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273054} {:answer/id #uuid "56a45d67-387d-5bbe-a775-e3b834b28aa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273125} {:answer/id #uuid "bfebdf4e-3dbb-5491-aac5-c1dd56989347" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274047} {:answer/id #uuid "cffa510c-caa9-52f5-a1e0-3c92d3101954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56a45d67-387d-5bbe-a775-e3b834b28aa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273125} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d5e25b-e9dd-5b67-a360-a93e26d946ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274948}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e8cb683f-c83e-5880-b481-a77cc9d224dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-158038" :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=3,$ then $tan⁡(-x)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "108e0db6-e384-55c4-99d1-574ed07b4c04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272535} {:answer/id #uuid "2d056441-eef5-508e-8a9a-a14472e5ee72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272787} {:answer/id #uuid "3408c295-dfdb-5479-b58d-4c80e71521e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272850} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb4819f-626e-504a-8ed6-1d686dc99901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273327} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b17a8c-3a77-587d-bb57-4517e5e4a287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "108e0db6-e384-55c4-99d1-574ed07b4c04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272535} :answer-field/id #uuid "5337af0e-e303-5162-b9ed-2cc7c66f30f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274774}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6710fdcd-157b-570c-95e4-ed16e27fba49" :question/math-academy-id "q-158041" :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=-4,$ then $\\frac{1}{2}tan⁡(-x+3\\pi )+3=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x+3\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-(-4) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}tan⁡(-x+3\\pi )+3 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}+3 \\\\  & =2+3 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4513555a-9108-5088-907d-beecce571174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272977} {:answer/id #uuid "87ddb98b-6f18-5542-9e00-38ccd036fdd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273552} {:answer/id #uuid "af5d1927-4709-58ef-bf27-8dace68c851c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273902} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb032b7-f1b2-5154-9e14-aba1200cc20d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274477} {:answer/id #uuid "fd56bc52-d5a9-5db6-8188-6c6b48c73474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4513555a-9108-5088-907d-beecce571174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272977} :answer-field/id #uuid "e257a236-bcb1-507a-a11a-a984805bc856" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275032}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e6dd4ab2-db9a-5355-9ce0-ec6183a9429b" :question/math-academy-id "q-158046" :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=-2,$ then $\\frac{3}{5}tan⁡(-x-7\\pi )+\\frac{4}{5}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x-7\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}tan⁡(-x-7\\pi )+\\frac{4}{5} & =\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot 2+\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Tangent Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176060 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175706 :question/id #uuid "12eb5678-b62b-50ad-97bf-88768c4bf365" :question/math-academy-id "e-11015" :question/problem "If $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{7}{2},$ then find the value of $\\csc(-x - 20\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y = \\csc{x}.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\csc{x}$ is $2\\pi.$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\csc{x}$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\csc(-x) = -\\csc{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\csc{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-20\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-2\\pi \\cdot 10) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\csc{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\csc(-x - 20\\pi) = -\\dfrac{7}{2}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a710f9-fed1-5fdb-b4c0-fd3ae2d4d8a4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d8fe6bb-05bc-53e2-b737-df277cb972b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 + 10π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174371} {:answer/id #uuid "d0072c09-102e-5f26-b9ec-ebe27de2b8b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6 + 10π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174372} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9128ef-d9fb-56b9-9d10-32dddd31731e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174373} {:answer/id #uuid "3f114e38-9266-52a3-803e-4957ec4d7ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174374} {:answer/id #uuid "45dc9571-4dde-552d-ba93-16d499d9486c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174375}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b59e176a-31b8-5614-805e-f290b228eb36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174370}] :question/id #uuid "a4506da5-1784-5af7-8323-f54b27402d05" :question/math-academy-id "q-136605" :question/problem "If $\\csc x =-6$, then $\\csc (-x + 10π) =$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ca3fea2-c3c6-5979-9638-f1e7771309d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174377} {:answer/id #uuid "944001cd-43eb-59fe-a455-3a1cabdf07d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174378} {:answer/id #uuid "3f739943-ca77-5424-bb7f-91810774321d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174379} {:answer/id #uuid "53e50c25-755b-5186-9198-428e552a0342" :answer/type {:db/ident :ans