torname{csc}⁡(-a)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-a) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡a \\\\  & =-(-3) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c7814cb-8a14-543c-b191-090e1d7ec186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272638} {:answer/id #uuid "912d0bf4-9153-5eaf-8129-ed93f063eca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273648} {:answer/id #uuid "979f59c9-f61f-5d15-890f-5f7e912c17f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273701} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c841e0-0d84-5ffb-afef-0ed443247d86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274456} {:answer/id #uuid "fb39b869-1aff-5ccd-a39a-e66bfc83a392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9c841e0-0d84-5ffb-afef-0ed443247d86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274456} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca6a7db3-5479-511f-aa28-280da1724856" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274989}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "990fed19-47aa-5e1c-b060-215e7bc1b3ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-158065" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡a=\\frac{11}{4},$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-a)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-a) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡a \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "292ad9c3-556f-503a-b08c-b44ad1d91bac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272755} {:answer/id #uuid "be923da4-a251-5258-8fd8-8ec7030d474e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274037} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d75b09-0328-5a2e-a2da-1ae52a79b49f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274220} {:answer/id #uuid "da3d157a-1571-5b60-9515-0964575b73cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274293} {:answer/id #uuid "f34776a9-7285-53c9-b3cc-2200945f2c4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "292ad9c3-556f-503a-b08c-b44ad1d91bac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272755} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7315967-925b-5233-bc2d-60b556baa525" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275069}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bd48781b-b5c4-5134-ac43-371c5870ba80" :question/math-academy-id "q-158066" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡a=\\sqrt{5},$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-a)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-a) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡a \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07122d60-244d-56e3-a00b-eb96f3716142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272449} {:answer/id #uuid "25672e79-fe59-50ab-8635-6d7d292513d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272715} {:answer/id #uuid "5e2d5e9a-aa6a-5731-9d0f-dfa77ea66bfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273196} {:answer/id #uuid "8af6a938-9009-5120-a929-4672c7317691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273586} {:answer/id #uuid "d4488d48-2270-5c1c-8f8a-587a9534610b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8af6a938-9009-5120-a929-4672c7317691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273586} :answer-field/id #uuid "a74777cf-30d2-57ad-a037-062e23d99586" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274931}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7d2f0215-ecc5-5f39-91e6-424fd801bf23" :question/math-academy-id "q-158069" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-4,$ then $3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-18\\pi )-7=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-18\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-2\\pi \\cdot 9) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\  & =-(-4) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-18\\pi )-7 & =3(4)-7 \\\\  & =12-7 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ce714d8-554b-5354-a70c-c88220f6cb42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273331} {:answer/id #uuid "a1025211-77b5-5b03-8606-c8562215708e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273783} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e066f7-00a4-56e3-bec2-634660c4b3dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273822} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0b140a-f05b-50c4-992b-d9f86cd91f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274189} {:answer/id #uuid "ed579fc1-af8b-5a45-be91-452681696d91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ce714d8-554b-5354-a70c-c88220f6cb42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273331} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbe91a72-b803-5369-bdce-e9dc3475245a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274992}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "52fb1c00-a770-5c09-ab79-cdf019b59f0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-158071" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{7}{6},$ then $4\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+6\\pi )+1=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+6\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+6\\pi )+1 & =4(-\\frac{7}{6})+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{14}{3}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179673 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179529 :question/id #uuid "1cadcaa9-876e-56cb-b8b3-17695827c4f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-7035" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7a6e67435aab40d5bf3630c66f79241008357e1549b1c77b4c0bdfea2b453883.png)\n\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C \\dfrac{y\\,\\mathrm{d}x - x\\,\\mathrm{d}y}{x^2+y^2},$ where $C$ is a part of the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $\\left(0,2\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that a parametrization for the given path is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{′}(t)\\,i+{y}^{′}(t)\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y\\,dx-x\\,dy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{y\\cdot {x}^{′}-x\\cdot {y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}},dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{-4{sin}^{2}⁡t-4{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{-4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}{4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,dt \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,dt \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5b5c517-8e13-5b70-bd1f-15ae495f0632" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180008 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02791ce3-0e59-59cb-a7c2-9c1f5f9b8efe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256804} {:answer/id #uuid "190adee9-da90-5ae0-b72f-1d54cbec3732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256997} {:answer/id #uuid "560421a3-f826-5226-b6d1-b81db072eecf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257522} {:answer/id #uuid "7390b29f-af98-55d8-908c-23b7a22da5d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257794} {:answer/id #uuid "b236cd5d-f995-5fc3-910f-4a9dd21013f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t+{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b236cd5d-f995-5fc3-910f-4a9dd21013f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t+{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258334} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c7b60de-da7e-5307-9f2b-34e67ae5cd00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259166}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec525dce-ff06-5bce-974f-75c3d14b6b37" :question/math-academy-id "q-124597" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx-{x}^{2}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(1,0)$ to the point $B(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx-{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({y}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}-{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-{sin}^{3}⁡t-{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t+{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50e89690-a473-5e96-8e1b-8772141217ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349180} {:answer/id #uuid "530d4756-be46-5443-808a-7585bbe7565c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349205} {:answer/id #uuid "6697a40a-e34b-5bb9-950b-de39a8750a4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349386} {:answer/id #uuid "921c24c0-b579-5b2b-8d7f-6191bf8532b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349754} {:answer/id #uuid "9aead026-b2ca-55fe-850d-efdababe6aa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "921c24c0-b579-5b2b-8d7f-6191bf8532b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349754} :answer-field/id #uuid "245516cf-1ad0-5aae-a56c-6eb5c3eb71f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350773}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "233cf6a6-4ea2-585c-8c92-fd68b4e1c273" :question/math-academy-id "q-81192" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{y\\,dy-x\\,dx}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y\\,dy-x\\,dx}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{y\\cdot {y}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t-x\\cdot {x}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{y\\cdot {y}^{′}-x\\cdot {x}^{′}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[-1-1] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f4f537-9c38-5a4a-92d9-1fc05c60f90d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256834} {:answer/id #uuid "3789ffb2-b7e7-5c19-9e46-46878e9dd27c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257270} {:answer/id #uuid "78bd4c3d-cccd-5c94-8184-aae6764245be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-2{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257849} {:answer/id #uuid "df481504-80af-5bb2-8167-dfe689532510" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t(1-4{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258727} {:answer/id #uuid "e25e2064-e1d7-50b0-b597-8c407a11946b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e25e2064-e1d7-50b0-b597-8c407a11946b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258751} :answer-field/id #uuid "046799fa-5877-570c-92f0-0954916f5fcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259039}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1324f71e-2d99-5801-9cdc-c3861396a17d" :question/math-academy-id "q-124656" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(-2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-16{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77262323-79ed-5ade-a88d-cfe620b7da6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257835} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f4a4ec-6fb3-5b3a-ace0-98c820d9456a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-1\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258256} {:answer/id #uuid "ce873cc3-03d3-531f-9ee9-9641247e9f3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+1\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258576} {:answer/id #uuid "da94ceb2-a1b8-5f7d-817e-d3c142cc2c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258692} {:answer/id #uuid "dd7f1f33-0822-5dd8-b24e-299b64b487d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-2\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8f4a4ec-6fb3-5b3a-ace0-98c820d9456a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-1\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258256} :answer-field/id #uuid "665ec369-2b3a-59ec-a54c-232cc0215c3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259209}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b45898cb-232a-5e7b-9fc7-75fcf27a9882" :question/math-academy-id "q-124657" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{2}{y}\\,dx+y{e}^{x}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(1,0)$ to the point $B(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{2}{y}\\,dx+y{e}^{x}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2}{y}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+y{e}^{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{2}{y}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+y{e}^{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-1\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259703 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f6d91d8-d6dd-52c9-b3ff-b1db44329a0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257620} {:answer/id #uuid "6266e64e-03c1-58e9-a2a7-faa1e9d1ab4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\pi -5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257649} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e0d134-f8c3-5cad-825a-eca5a50c39ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi +1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258774} {:answer/id #uuid "eef0270e-6352-5435-b70f-a6965924b8db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\pi -5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258853} {:answer/id #uuid "fddbbdf7-66ee-5acd-97fd-6d8474436d61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6266e64e-03c1-58e9-a2a7-faa1e9d1ab4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\pi -5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257649} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4c70434-b863-58cf-ba58-7f3a3ae42742" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259432}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3189421a-c22a-5fa2-87db-a7498ce87212" :question/math-academy-id "q-124661" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(0,3)$ to the point $(-2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{′}(t)\\,i+{y}^{′}(t)\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(x-y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(x+y)\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }((x-y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+(x+y)\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6{sin}^{2}⁡t+6{cos}^{2}⁡t+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+6\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+6t]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[(-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )+6\\pi )-(-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+3\\pi )] \\\\  & =(-\\frac{5}{4}+6\\pi )-(\\frac{5}{4}+3\\pi ) \\\\  & =3\\pi -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi -5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259706 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c3f67e-c143-52cf-8d04-0883a4d5a256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256943} {:answer/id #uuid "5dfb3475-8c2e-59e8-84f2-1ebef1519964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257605} {:answer/id #uuid "79b7785e-2e75-5831-91d7-a32ea05f26cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257857} {:answer/id #uuid "a627c339-0874-52fc-ab69-4b8d01f804f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258229} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c4c7b4-80c1-5190-8730-555b2288af36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi +1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a627c339-0874-52fc-ab69-4b8d01f804f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258229} :answer-field/id #uuid "39dbfdbb-3be4-5981-9a19-60472431c20b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259140}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e2357f5-0311-5b8a-87b7-eaae763c04c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-124665" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}dx+\\frac{2}{x}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(-1,0)$ to the point $(0,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}dx+\\frac{2}{x}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{2}{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2}{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6t]_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+9\\pi )-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +6\\pi ) \\\\  & =(0+9\\pi )-(-1+6\\pi ) \\\\  & =3\\pi +1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387533 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2f1d0a0-f937-5c5a-a906-b8d8c3476536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387535} {:answer/id #uuid "b7352c23-717f-5780-b0d0-69655ec1cf5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387536} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9c4e1a-9de0-5b54-9a84-bd61bf918f52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387537} {:answer/id #uuid "8321670f-4321-5b31-a978-0d03d440b8cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387538} {:answer/id #uuid "6b7e8a42-2b6d-59b6-8e6b-34f626db24de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa9c4e1a-9de0-5b54-9a84-bd61bf918f52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387537} :answer-field/id #uuid "46e3edf1-c65d-5b40-9b5a-4947c9652286" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387534}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "35a5240c-df73-5f77-b045-605d4d50b078" :question/math-academy-id "q-124651" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,dx+xy\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $1,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,dx+xy\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+xy\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+xy\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-8{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387540 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e75bdd4-9ffa-598b-ba2b-3e4718966455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+3\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387542} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b5e560-c25e-5180-91e9-4dd26cebdf25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387543} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5e8954-0ec8-5e2c-87e8-00cd2ba07083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387544} {:answer/id #uuid "e56c1b23-1bc1-5e37-920c-d6b5c4c83b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+1\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387545} {:answer/id #uuid "96172456-0f12-5f8e-95b8-f6b631ca3655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+3\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96172456-0f12-5f8e-95b8-f6b631ca3655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+3\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387546} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f4769e4-af1d-5493-a3fe-c583f362931e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387541}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8b59c59-a390-5b22-8421-70bf95742c06" :question/math-academy-id "q-124653" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}({e}^{x}+y)\\,dx-x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(0,\\sqrt{3})$ to the point $B(-\\sqrt{3},0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({e}^{x}+y)\\,dx-x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }({e}^{x}+y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(({e}^{x}+y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}-x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }({e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-3{sin}^{2}⁡t-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+3\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57571e56-49bb-5855-a1ea-93ab592af29a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}6{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387549} {:answer/id #uuid "dee85688-f7f4-5420-abcb-2c8946c6128c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387550} {:answer/id #uuid "581fc888-b8c4-5b98-a662-97d6df2c1c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}6-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387551} {:answer/id #uuid "0fa25052-4bcd-5645-84a9-cb44c291ba75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387552} {:answer/id #uuid "179cd5d2-4e71-5c6d-adbe-d8d33387b74f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}3-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "581fc888-b8c4-5b98-a662-97d6df2c1c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}6-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387551} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab73ef72-cd0a-53ca-954d-b2e9e89157ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387548}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4adcf258-fc9a-5300-865b-c8efb5f76002" :question/math-academy-id "q-124655" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(x-2y)\\,dx+2x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(0,3)$ to the point $B(0,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-2y)\\,dx+2x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(x-2y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+2x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}((x-2y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+2x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6{sin}^{2}⁡t+6{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}6-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f548fd7-8b35-5ec3-9cec-fbc79515f307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415406} {:answer/id #uuid "68411306-3576-579b-8a21-34c1ab7c2b84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415416} {:answer/id #uuid "910075a4-9923-557c-b9e3-c1e2ff793ed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415422} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d88b10-d2ad-5c06-9db7-9b7365276f6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415426} {:answer/id #uuid "f7735ce7-7b0f-5b5a-82f6-7e753a067e1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f548fd7-8b35-5ec3-9cec-fbc79515f307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415406} :answer-field/id #uuid "0df18aab-7e84-55d8-803f-e0704648d1b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415433}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "228970be-ca08-547c-9937-b7e76a0b4145" :question/math-academy-id "q-81186" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $B(0,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the parametrization of the curve $C.$ Since it's a circle if radius $2$ centered at the origin, the curve $C$ can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve we have that $x(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(-4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t{|}_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )] \\\\  & =2(0-0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "256764ed-4369-50dc-930e-662876f022a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415404} {:answer/id #uuid "4130f50c-9761-535e-832c-969370eba9c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415410} {:answer/id #uuid "56de169b-e6cc-53f1-9778-d57c45a41155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415413} {:answer/id #uuid "72db92dd-d3bb-5244-9ae0-06d36401d8d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415417} {:answer/id #uuid "fb33e709-cf30-5cd2-9a8f-17fc862403a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72db92dd-d3bb-5244-9ae0-06d36401d8d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415417} :answer-field/id #uuid "b64329ed-5836-53c3-881a-5ef3bf67996e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415438}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26043436-d017-5e07-ac9c-a4df40dc0429" :question/math-academy-id "q-90895" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}y\\,dx-x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(\\sqrt{2},0)$ to the point $B(0,\\sqrt{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the parametrization of the curve $C.$ Since it's a circle if radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin, the curve $C$ can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve we have that $x(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx-x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}-x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-2{sin}^{2}⁡t-2{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2t{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-2[\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0] \\\\  & =-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Circles and Ellipses"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179674 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179530 :question/id #uuid "5a63ca22-0b1a-5584-8b43-c80457016cd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-7038" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=x^2$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1),$ evaluate the line integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x + (x-y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y = x^2.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(x-y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 1+(t-{t}^{2})\\cdot 2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t+3{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+{t}^{3}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+1-\\frac{1}{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49e57c7d-ef79-5439-be59-40bbdc9b3e0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415412} {:answer/id #uuid "7cadedbb-d8e2-5bdf-97a6-9ad1eb1ceece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415420} {:answer/id #uuid "c58f6ab5-55eb-56af-a44a-0c359a236b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415424} {:answer/id #uuid "dc50983f-8d67-57c5-a859-8dc7f564511a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415427} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c20e31-578e-5df9-8b7f-fd828cbb1b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9c20e31-578e-5df9-8b7f-fd828cbb1b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415429} :answer-field/id #uuid "55008b23-7b99-5d0d-87f9-a75bcea2d1c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415434}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93a8c36b-e0d3-5d9c-9527-8161a7655e20" :question/math-academy-id "q-39344" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(2,4),$ then ${\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+y\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+y\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\cdot 1+{t}^{2}\\cdot 2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}t+2{t}^{3}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =2+8 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180286 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22633649-e91e-5f15-bfbd-fcb4a483affb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348742} {:answer/id #uuid "3f792e82-8361-5835-a14b-ecd98c471f19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349024} {:answer/id #uuid "9e65a7c0-308a-5d44-8737-c54996be3dae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349860} {:answer/id #uuid "bc1e3e20-823b-5b23-90e3-b832e595b63a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350119} {:answer/id #uuid "f37cb0c3-6210-59b3-8ea9-2f0e5f7a13b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f792e82-8361-5835-a14b-ecd98c471f19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349024} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcb520da-e760-5dc7-8812-5ec17ba0763e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351090}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2bdab86-46e0-5604-a07d-20d0ff29dcb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81178" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=(x+1)^{2}$ traversed from the point $P(0,1)$ to point $Q(1,4),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{dx+dy}{y}=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=(x+1)^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+(t+1)^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2(t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{dx+dy}{y} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1+2(t+1)}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{t+1}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{t+1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{1+1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+1)]-[-\\frac{1}{0+1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)] \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2]-[-1+0] \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13fcc3d9-e48a-542e-9d30-6b397fb1a498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256961} {:answer/id #uuid "1869c282-abb4-524c-be28-650f45a1ebb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{53}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256994} {:answer/id #uuid "3697d3f0-d2a9-5157-9c3a-73b0ea60e2e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257260} {:answer/id #uuid "4a0cfb60-19ef-5381-83bb-81e635e37ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257429} {:answer/id #uuid "b332b886-e893-52d6-b7df-0c964a1cd0c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{42}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1869c282-abb4-524c-be28-650f45a1ebb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{53}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256994} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f64dde8-4609-5f96-b95a-2b0b8b027fbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259282}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bbd34e81-f803-5fbc-b7c5-b362f720fe60" :question/math-academy-id "q-124664" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=2\\sqrt{x}$ traversed from the point $P(1,2)$ to point $Q(4,4),$ then ${\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=2\\sqrt{x}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j={t}^{2}\\,i+2t\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2t\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}((x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot 2t+({t}^{2}+2t)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}t({t}^{2}-2t)+({t}^{2}+2t)\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+{t}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2[(\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}+{2}^{2})-(\\frac{{1}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}+{1}^{2})] \\\\  & =2[4-\\frac{8}{3}+4-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{3}-1] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{53}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{53}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259707 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "181e2f70-d760-5c6f-a30f-585047479128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256990} {:answer/id #uuid "381edb78-eaaa-5872-8c1d-fd2478651f4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257275} {:answer/id #uuid "55d0b7e0-71bf-594a-a93e-ca2764905f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257521} {:answer/id #uuid "71b1c76c-c17a-5ae5-a386-f4803282a9b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257780} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9bb5b2-b51a-5f5b-9ec6-58471882be92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55d0b7e0-71bf-594a-a93e-ca2764905f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257521} :answer-field/id #uuid "039596a1-9fe5-54ad-8161-f20c3677fd50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259037}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "170a6ced-a411-5130-91d2-063d0a1b4a48" :question/math-academy-id "q-124668" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y={x}^{3}$ traversed from the point $P(1,1)$ to point $Q(2,8),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{3}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+{t}^{3}\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+3{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}}+3t]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})}+3(2)]-[-\\frac{1}{2({1}^{2})}+3(1)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}+6+\\frac{1}{2}-3 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326776 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30e5bd11-b4a5-5c3f-97c8-baf947fdb648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324361} {:answer/id #uuid "ce09c054-3fce-534d-ab95-741a3083a7f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325760} {:answer/id #uuid "dc484259-e0ad-507c-9124-07e7bfbf313a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325892} {:answer/id #uuid "ea5488aa-cd87-5a5b-9265-c03f48ae0f85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326037} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9c2e80-2a3e-55ba-bc7f-3b7949f67a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea5488aa-cd87-5a5b-9265-c03f48ae0f85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326037} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2f3e9a2-5330-5b08-8e51-b27a94666da3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326509}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12ff35df-51f6-5e85-9109-59feb724214e" :question/math-academy-id "q-39679" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ traversed from the point $P(0,0)$ to point $Q(1,1),$ then ${\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{xy}\\,dx+dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j={t}^{3}\\,i+t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=3{t}^{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{xy}\\,dx+dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4y{e}^{xy}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4y{e}^{xy}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4t\\cdot {e}^{{t}^{3}\\cdot t}\\cdot 3{t}^{2}+1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12{t}^{3}{e}^{{t}^{4}}+1\\,dt \\\\  & ={[3{e}^{{t}^{4}}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[3{e}^{(1)^{4}}+(1)]-[3{e}^{(0)^{4}}+(0)] \\\\  & =[3e+1]-[3] \\\\  & =3e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351189 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f3db826-20ca-540b-9b96-6dc6ed206e9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348865} {:answer/id #uuid "43f3642f-8a9e-5fee-a6d8-eabf284c8f26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349065} {:answer/id #uuid "47e75774-fac8-5062-880a-727a16ba9eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349096} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecbe21c-5609-5fa1-ba0f-81eb95a13511" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349608} {:answer/id #uuid "ae441ea5-b6f0-508a-83ed-e409f76a2a51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae441ea5-b6f0-508a-83ed-e409f76a2a51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349987} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf326210-69ad-557c-8a58-7a89edc90759" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351042}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd7813dc-b09d-5c4d-8539-6d09e01430cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-80983" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=\\sqrt{x}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,1),$ then  ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+y)\\,dx+(3x+2y)\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j={t}^{2}\\,i+t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2t\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+y)\\,dx+(3x+2y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(3x+2y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(3x+2y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({({t}^{2})}^{2}+t)\\cdot 2t+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+t)\\cdot 2t+3{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{t}^{5}+5{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5{t}^{3}}{3}+{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{(1)^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5(1)^{3}}{3}+(1)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}+1]-[0] \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17becf89-3701-509a-a9a8-440484064ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348648} {:answer/id #uuid "20742a72-7769-5476-9ce9-3b1881e0470b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348727} {:answer/id #uuid "47810cbc-2cf1-5cf8-8896-58252baa012e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349094} {:answer/id #uuid "ee29d359-0faa-5f3d-aacd-2a4fd062f399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350529} {:answer/id #uuid "f83e5f36-bde0-573a-91de-bf6ecdf50c44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47810cbc-2cf1-5cf8-8896-58252baa012e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349094} :answer-field/id #uuid "81d0252a-6745-5c15-b97f-a22408b67a5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350931}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a071a2f-be85-5b88-9507-97c63159bbb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-81098" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y={x}^{2}-x$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(2,2),$ then ${\\int }_{C}2xy\\,dx+x\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{2}-x.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+({t}^{2}-t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+(2t-1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2xy\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2t({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot 1+t(2t-1)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{t}^{3}-t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =8-2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5480a9aa-ad9e-5901-99ef-a4b557010682" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349219} {:answer/id #uuid "9515cda8-658c-596e-82bc-d1176ae74422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349782} {:answer/id #uuid "d708ba15-3640-5e0f-a2cc-8efebb555009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350330} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a9cd99-2cbe-59b5-8edc-4b78ef09dc96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+14\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350495} {:answer/id #uuid "f55e6f64-fa6a-5856-9b77-a53e676b505a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9a9cd99-2cbe-59b5-8edc-4b78ef09dc96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+14\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350495} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ed4da5c-67f9-5122-bb2a-f6a68757af78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350790}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bbe13972-64bc-5d76-9613-e21318b0221e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81180" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=\\pi {x}^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(1,\\pi )$ to point $B(2,4\\pi ),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}})\\,dx+3x\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\pi {x}^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+\\pi {t}^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}})\\,dx+3x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+3x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+3x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{2{t}^{2}})\\cdot 1+3t\\cdot 2\\pi t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+6\\pi {t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}1+6\\pi {t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & =[t+2\\pi {t}^{3}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[2+2\\pi (2)^{3}]-[1+2\\pi (1)^{3}] \\\\  & =[2+16\\pi ]-[1+2\\pi ] \\\\  & =1+14\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be1f2782-b94c-5338-8044-608f3468b85f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{40}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356328} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6d80f7-dfb7-512a-abad-766ac81adffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356439} {:answer/id #uuid "d5012490-41ad-5072-9d60-53f86f6f37ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356527} {:answer/id #uuid "d7358768-6a5a-5876-8e36-c2f97c5a588a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356546} {:answer/id #uuid "e6036eac-887e-5728-bc69-99a208e6785a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb6d80f7-dfb7-512a-abad-766ac81adffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356439} :answer-field/id #uuid "1281a916-cca9-5586-a7c5-f8adff0691eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356950}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d77bf889-9285-57c2-9d70-8b359a57d0fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-90848" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y={x}^{2}-1$ traversed from the point $A(0,-1)$ to point $B(2,3),$ then ${\\int }_{C}5{x}^{2}y\\,dx+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{2}-1.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}5{x}^{2}y\\,dx+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(5{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\cdot 1+{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-1+1)\\cdot 2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{t}^{4}-5{t}^{2}+2{t}^{5}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[{t}^{5}-\\frac{5}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{{t}^{6}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[{2}^{5}-\\frac{5}{3}(2)^{3}+\\frac{{2}^{6}}{3}]-[0] \\\\  & =32-\\frac{40}{3}+\\frac{64}{3} \\\\  & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b5a7777-a54c-5144-9dd2-6db2bdfadf58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355029} {:answer/id #uuid "61232685-a7a4-54c0-b2f7-04ab7b7daab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355527} {:answer/id #uuid "9e198969-77e7-54c7-a4d5-8fcaa9d1d45a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356073} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d4dda7-ea67-59f3-a16d-acadacf7c5e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356127} {:answer/id #uuid "aaae3ce5-d578-5c1b-a2fb-4ead0dd1fc64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61232685-a7a4-54c0-b2f7-04ab7b7daab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355527} :answer-field/id #uuid "331df704-6bf7-5c71-9966-44b1422cc8d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356999}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "067b683a-f3df-5f66-b795-e612d7f9ac62" :question/math-academy-id "q-90871" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=\\pi {x}^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,\\pi ),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})\\,dx+4{x}^{2}\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\pi {x}^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+\\pi {t}^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})\\,dx+4{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+4{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+4{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}})\\cdot 1+4{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\pi t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +8\\pi {t}^{3}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}8\\pi {t}^{3}-1\\,dt \\\\  & =[2\\pi {t}^{4}-t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[2\\pi (1)^{4}-1]-[0] \\\\  & =2\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "225888f9-79d1-51ec-b69e-df715e48a48c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415403} {:answer/id #uuid "3341cdec-88e2-5336-a6c4-1be4742e22df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415408} {:answer/id #uuid "5b61e1f7-a8be-5897-ab62-f5700cfc5da5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415414} {:answer/id #uuid "7872d0b7-7e4c-5866-a2c5-fa62a0852ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415418} {:answer/id #uuid "ff788f7f-efa0-5800-af8c-7f4c47429255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff788f7f-efa0-5800-af8c-7f4c47429255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415432} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac11d5c1-82f1-564e-be01-8e20a8e6d4bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415436}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ee6b1fc-a5dc-5685-b3c9-ad25e0e1efc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-124666" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y={x}^{2}-1$ traversed from the point $P(1,0)$ to point $Q(2,3),$ then ${\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+x\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{2}-1.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{({t}^{2}-1)}^{2}\\cdot 1+t\\cdot 2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+1+2{t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{4}+1\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}+t]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}+2]-[\\frac{{1}^{5}}{5}+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5}+2-\\frac{1}{5}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179709 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179571 :question/id #uuid "4088c5c5-6f69-54f3-9029-3ba52f5b00d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9435" :question/problem "![](images/2e/2e4e808d8264685f9b88c5b0052870174d8be3ed2a7195149d45552118186a49.png)\n\nBy setting $y = t$ where $t$ is a parameter, find a definite integral with respect to $t$ that is equivalent to the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} \\dfrac{x}{y} \\, \\textrm ds,$ where $C$ is the arc of the parabola $x = y^2$ traversed between the points $(1,1)$ and $(9 , 3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y = t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx = x(t) = t^2.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t^2 , \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1 \\leq t \\leq 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9471e9d-8fd8-54f6-b2a2-21979387ec44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^{3}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177418} {:answer/id #uuid "cfaf38e8-dea0-53ca-af4c-7595b6aa19d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^{3}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177419} {:answer/id #uuid "da81cbb7-1c0d-5fb6-a136-436cce0614d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^{3}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177420} {:answer/id #uuid "1a417b80-1c7d-54df-a4b9-d83760ab35a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^{2}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177421} {:answer/id #uuid "548acf66-02a4-5f9b-bf62-28fc428f02f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^{2}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177422}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7770a3af-79d6-5207-9481-e0e095e0784b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177417}] :question/id #uuid "4163da76-7dc5-52f8-8a6d-5dcabcafe172" :question/math-academy-id "q-122139" :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$\\displaystyle \\int_(C)32xyds =-\\frac{1}{4}\\displaystyle \\int_{-8}^{4}AAAAAdt$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $y^{2} =-16x$ traversed from the point $(-4,-8)$ to the point $(-1, 4)$. Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y = t$.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cfcc5e2-5fae-5e85-98a0-4b4a8389615e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{36t^{4} + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177484} {:answer/id #uuid "b60f042f-515e-55c8-9669-504d7122f51c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}t^{5}\\sqrt{36t^{4} + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177485} {:answer/id #uuid "fa827bf5-62aa-53a4-8c0b-0bb5bf842b23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{16}\\sqrt{36t^{4} + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177486} {:answer/id #uuid "275b43e3-32aa-59d3-b6f6-d5224224ccce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}t^{5}\\sqrt{t^{4} + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177487} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6793c3-401c-517a-8cdb-2cd95d932197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{16}t^{5}\\sqrt{36t^{4} + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177488}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6218cae7-f715-5e6e-b9dc-dfe12bb38c53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177483}] :question/id #uuid "8b50d48f-8fc4-5b58-ae9a-175e59cd59d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-122446" :question/problem "![](images/dd/ddefdc9ada8270723d9be7ab7fe3f0b2a59b73fbe69d38b674f2fa5c43800e4c.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)xy^{2}ds$, where $C$ is the section of the curve $x = 2y^{3}$ traversed between the points $(0, 0)$ and $(16, 2)$, as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y = t$.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256656 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7542f2f6-b892-5626-afb8-db34cd70e0c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254709} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c6ea31-331c-56c0-bd88-8b436b2b7eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255170} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ea6601-5749-54c9-ba36-44e09b3b0dea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{3}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255681} {:answer/id #uuid "e7df80cd-42a0-564e-887c-f1473180be67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255709} {:answer/id #uuid "f5478718-e38f-51df-ba4d-0a7c66983c0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9c6ea31-331c-56c0-bd88-8b436b2b7eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255170} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffffbcf7-2290-5771-97a7-2e3fca38c6aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256369}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c37df0ad-2178-59ae-82b8-7b50bbb1ab58" :question/math-academy-id "q-122530" :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}9{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{9}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $3x=y$ traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(3,9).$ Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n3x=y\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{t}{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{3},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 9\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 9$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{3})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{10}{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=9{x}^{2}y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9{(\\frac{t}{3})}^{2}(t) \\\\  & =9(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{9})(t) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}9{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{9}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256657 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d0041b6-b7a6-544b-a5a1-4601e83eb423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253911} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba4acff-ed4d-5004-82bf-b89074e988ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{e}^{2e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254189} {:answer/id #uuid "76b7eee7-6a99-5701-a4bd-ca2f9ad5ab1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{e}^{2e}t\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254718} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd7ab28-aada-5ff7-ae14-4e7c1b105102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255041} {:answer/id #uuid "f299ef39-a3f8-5921-9d4d-66255a05f5b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}t\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d0041b6-b7a6-544b-a5a1-4601e83eb423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253911} :answer-field/id #uuid "98b25ddd-1b34-5ba6-b53a-dc771bfba0c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256201}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2f457a6-0776-5f23-8cdb-bf0ad17c2cda" :question/math-academy-id "q-122534" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6cabe5598368b4f26ccca515d4f4011100e0bc410b72d919db9681b3145814fb.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(2,{e}^{2}),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{t})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1+{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{|t|} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)={e}^{x}{y}^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}){t}^{2} \\\\  & =(t){t}^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b799c33-4069-5d75-9acf-2c1fb425b3b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272775} {:answer/id #uuid "5eec3c9c-1f5e-50d8-ad72-41bdf8012479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273202} {:answer/id #uuid "8a45bf0f-9d2b-5f0b-8d67-58b93e277d83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273578} {:answer/id #uuid "9885a09f-ec2b-514c-a625-30bef7ad1a3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273711} {:answer/id #uuid "d1aedd3b-9487-5b78-b830-5dd393643e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1aedd3b-9487-5b78-b830-5dd393643e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274218} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac0269b0-7a87-54a9-bbbd-f720ed0c881a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274938}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba25d72c-66e5-5660-8cd6-858347d6e9a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-149714" :question/problem "![](images/28/2860103dc3d1019cddcffa504b3cce1fb8a84cfd046bc796712d1e16516d5269.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $\\frac{x}{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(2,e),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,⟹\\,x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le e\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le e$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2}{t})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4y{e}^{2x}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4y{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =4t{e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t} \\\\  & =4t({t}^{4}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(4{t}^{5})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c793c767-8ecf-564e-af97-7c0e5166064b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1/3}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+8}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387217} {:answer/id #uuid "71c2e31f-1ce7-5e4f-bb9e-8d3e5faf7e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387218} {:answer/id #uuid "b13d0e88-37bf-5316-8237-40c1c7c1a505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{1/3}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387219} {:answer/id #uuid "ae35c1a8-9ade-537f-b9ff-8d9b8d021811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387220} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a101a7-ad66-5ad3-8847-9c8591f60fb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+8}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae35c1a8-9ade-537f-b9ff-8d9b8d021811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387220} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4412e87-ff23-50d1-8bf7-efc4e936bf02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387216}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3bf8f8b4-ea1a-5394-a193-4b55a8b9d41a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122542" :question/problem "![](images/62/62f33dab4305a41a0bc180231fef7015361dec4aac63f14c31fd48a39b8fcd68.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{2}{y}$ traversed between the points $(2,1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{3},6),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{2}{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{t},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 6\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 6$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{t})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{4}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4+{t}^{4}}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4x{y}^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4(\\frac{2}{t})(t)^{2} \\\\  & =8t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}8t\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1875e854-1900-58b4-bea8-ec0e5d6629b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-9t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398230} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca1dcb5-771f-59eb-81e9-1e595c34e462" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398231} {:answer/id #uuid "676387be-6610-5f16-9796-2babd5ce0f03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398232} {:answer/id #uuid "ddbb623b-497e-5583-a206-85cecb99534f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398233} {:answer/id #uuid "a43b3ff9-d167-5f2a-a49d-342a3fc83921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1875e854-1900-58b4-bea8-ec0e5d6629b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-9t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398230} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d025741-a121-5bf5-a673-350aefe4b1cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398229}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "757cf839-4729-500e-9a5f-1ad15eb1165b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122527" :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}(2x-3y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $\\frac{x}{2}=1-\\frac{3y}{2}$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(-4,2).$ Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=1-\\frac{3y}{2}\\,⟹\\,x=2-3y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2-3t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2-3t,\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-3\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-3)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2x-3y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(2-3t)-3t \\\\  & =4-9t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2x-3y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-9t)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-9t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179712 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179574 :question/id #uuid "d83c960d-aeb2-5c31-b26f-f17518220ac0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9444" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4d5f211c3bf3d306c6dc14205311c60eeaaf55de89f8af571ec0fa9e5add8a6.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the above diagrams could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = xy?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -1,$ we get $xy = -1,$ which gives the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 1x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 0,$ we get $xy = 0,$ that is, the lines $y=0$ and $x=0$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 1,$ we get $xy =1,$ that is, the hyperbola $y=\\dfrac 1x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -2,$ we get $xy =-2,$ that is, the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 2x$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/f9/f95c7ab30b7b02d67ceec039792e649a900dbece2896cc1e63341fcba9fc794f.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move from left to right in the upper half-plane $y > 0,$ and increases as we move from right-to-left in the lower half-plane $y < 0.$\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/3d/3d6be27e083bb2e8b508cca31671e5cb4ea4e39b366a99f1dd194a5ea8ffcd3b.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/99/996b5678396b4e9a91a4c55697442895331a7a6e4f8ec3eed550ea86845e44f4.png)\n\n       \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is III."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "364d2c9a-6e9a-57f8-aa57-c10ee223061b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179967 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b73086dc-5509-5507-97a5-c7e3bfba2349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177472} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c2aec3-ab10-5812-a6f0-e32442afa1bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2c/2c01770dd5222fc43a65dd92381917821c127d39a0055ca464582c829ef8d3fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177473} {:answer/id #uuid "cad68cfa-0e83-5847-b359-8bce8928018f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c4/c4fa5e55308b46106812c7a3c2620b528487b520ea282144cc6baa2fd3cb345b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177474} {:answer/id #uuid "f4af8d52-9d50-500b-9881-dac55517541e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/417f9bd99ad7032ffac7ce258c5122d932b1bb1610237ef030f077ca56815bec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177475} {:answer/id #uuid "3ec2d219-5a14-5c00-a4d6-e94fc02507d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8e30bba74fad98713f891950fd052d2ddd2cb2dc349bd9bcb7efe3333b7203dd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b73086dc-5509-5507-97a5-c7e3bfba2349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177472} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fd1fc14-019a-5f80-944a-ad3518a49e16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177471}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43a11e4f-11c8-502a-b54b-73b03e51f3ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-122413" :question/problem "![](images/11/11c759098d959c7dbdcb81686640bfc6a01d4dc0f9b12e6ff45f3028ddc4d2db.png)\n\nThe gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\nObserve that the given field vectors are perpendicular to the level curves in the following picture.\n\n![](images/4b/4b20823340d89e0ff4aa2fd65078d3819e8b218419a54ea632c1109e698858ab.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/14/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "175c18fc-c927-5f10-aba9-f8c07cd1f45e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/22/229bb0ca171a2f77f16e15471bce4037e642299e7141025bfb178a81f901ee02.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253871} {:answer/id #uuid "4b15e5a9-3b6b-5a1d-98c3-3f8c03d6c410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cf/cf6f0f97f5b54b95519319242e4f3867ae2ac0edafd5e2ab6debdfba3e519401.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254316} {:answer/id #uuid "81f53b31-d962-5549-8632-88d0cfde19be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d55feb791b51d0b4ebfe6b8998f1aad1e1a8d57d1593097b0c99d706a01d47a8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254822} {:answer/id #uuid "c16229c8-fca2-5a01-8eeb-0140284c0d13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/37/372482df951dbb50d6db837939a0708a9a69ea330c03e59bce19398575ad50f7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255395} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc85735-898d-52d1-b585-43d59e88f534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c3/c3f79f33b0a983f7c92acebaddbd3bc5208275cca585ac5464ddf44c9a7e8d87.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b15e5a9-3b6b-5a1d-98c3-3f8c03d6c410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cf/cf6f0f97f5b54b95519319242e4f3867ae2ac0edafd5e2ab6debdfba3e519401.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254316} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ac1c634-c613-5135-9d7d-f12696fc2a00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256028}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50f7bc31-3a53-5257-80a4-a0a85f0ea7fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-122572" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0,$ which gives the origin $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=1,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ a circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=4,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/14/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move away from the origin.\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/4b/4b20823340d89e0ff4aa2fd65078d3819e8b218419a54ea632c1109e698858ab.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/cf/cf6f0f97f5b54b95519319242e4f3867ae2ac0edafd5e2ab6debdfba3e519401.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256718 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fb80c1c-95cb-5565-a077-e01dae2538e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254362} {:answer/id #uuid "5575b773-fb45-568e-9e09-8e292ff7268e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6598c50e72417a0bff5514fa2b8ff9fba7c617cff10dc83e15472259d040846.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254415} {:answer/id #uuid "9d45adbb-a2ba-5573-a768-bab8773e9e44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8e30bba74fad98713f891950fd052d2ddd2cb2dc349bd9bcb7efe3333b7203dd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255052} {:answer/id #uuid "bb73aff5-0f60-5f0e-a385-48945789fb36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/417f9bd99ad7032ffac7ce258c5122d932b1bb1610237ef030f077ca56815bec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255341} {:answer/id #uuid "f66620e0-e12e-5bd1-b12e-36f3f0afecb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9c/9ce2dd3e0c639e4f520556c7f4955451eb599b35e0714aa326cb6efd74cfaa5c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f66620e0-e12e-5bd1-b12e-36f3f0afecb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9c/9ce2dd3e0c639e4f520556c7f4955451eb599b35e0714aa326cb6efd74cfaa5c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255826} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d74b99d-539a-5a25-adea-708b48ca371b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256098}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef1fc6b0-b283-5ecf-84c4-88b300df87f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-122796" :question/problem "![](images/20/201804549f48637076fcaad5252632527b937e01cc75c153bc1db26b246d2aa6.png)\n\nThe gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\nObserve that the given field vectors are perpendicular to the level curves in the following picture.\n\n![](images/f4/f4dc7eaf7ed7651ee38157c486f185ef3eecaf9ccb7e91df18e897c56c5c5e23.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/9c/9ce2dd3e0c639e4f520556c7f4955451eb599b35e0714aa326cb6efd74cfaa5c.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00678095-4785-5a48-bfd2-04906406b23d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8bd5b7575d1693935df0d25a259767f0dd267fc1153139cd55cebad75e3988fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253671} {:answer/id #uuid "03aaca50-6ba1-5767-8a1d-61289921be5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4c/4c1a0035b37bc0137dc2ac164c980d538f9362feb3b439e663885f8aa7178124.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253699} {:answer/id #uuid "5936829d-c5a5-5317-93a8-8d046c07d07f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fa/fa8bf282264f1647644cc15b6aece745718bd17617afad2c2530292bfed10b15.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254452} {:answer/id #uuid "70f6299c-8313-5e6f-a9c7-80abdfba5fd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/70/704a1e0712c0c2512e16ac03f1eeb7ecd55996c822eced54ac510f4719485c42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254671} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf9db65-02b1-5896-a31b-6345273456e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f9/f9d6304bc9cc6b6e74235c66a7d70b96dbea2db44e9ed82606f6025075b76336.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00678095-4785-5a48-bfd2-04906406b23d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8bd5b7575d1693935df0d25a259767f0dd267fc1153139cd55cebad75e3988fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253671} :answer-field/id #uuid "a46a2d28-3dee-5856-b9b2-7ac4fb2b706b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256221}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f67d0b79-608f-51fc-afe3-9623997cda39" :question/math-academy-id "q-122810" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=-1,$ which gives the parabola $y={x}^{2}+1$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=0,$ that is, the parabola $y={x}^{2}$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=2,$ that is, the parabola $y={x}^{2}-2$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/77/77a54ef31c41e0246e3d337a69c7faa161194058e6a5c672ef24d86866a83faf.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move downward and away from the origin $O.$ \n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/4e/4e124d5e77ef9a28002aba9aac40f0b7eff1232dd1c08b6bca10b166469675c7.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n \n\n![](images/8b/8bd5b7575d1693935df0d25a259767f0dd267fc1153139cd55cebad75e3988fd.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d16f010-8927-5a11-821b-dadc53e436d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3d/3d5e0a4e1a1784167984a6dde093b97b14ebb0910ce56cd1d879782bfad6b7e8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253779} {:answer/id #uuid "118abcd0-7890-5af2-b48d-3e8745d6c268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3f/3fc34a2294e81abf04bfacf7d4367c0492a173af45f8f453a86b95a3a6028d4d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253822} {:answer/id #uuid "6523a047-fedb-5a31-a148-d33413134901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b6/b6b8dc1bcaf76374a580505308a9282b65e5da1b7d0961e2a6c30ba8ce6b2dd4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254577} {:answer/id #uuid "716fad33-4080-5fc3-bc31-939e1cf8b681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cb/cb66a357917b9683a61b04aeab03ff60e359160f3c5e51dc4b282d6464666467.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254676} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff449c6-cc37-5b79-8ef9-32639dce72bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5f/5fd011917170ef1f098b875060ad48d3ea5d33a1d16d369ae58614c53cd29c9c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ff449c6-cc37-5b79-8ef9-32639dce72bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5f/5fd011917170ef1f098b875060ad48d3ea5d33a1d16d369ae58614c53cd29c9c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254934} :answer-field/id #uuid "e493ba4b-c644-55d9-8213-f4543a4426f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256327}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d7e9bf88-89a8-589d-8711-7c4a07b86d32" :question/math-academy-id "q-122829" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=0,$ which gives the origin $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=1,$ we get $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which gives an ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $1$ centered at $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=4,$ we get $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=4,$ which gives an ellipse with semiaxes $4$ and $2$ centered at $O$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/83/835b4206e04d064485d02635cfd4e2ad39a934b1d2b38296cc6463a265f5d931.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move away from the origin.\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/cd/cd8e0a10d162d30be895fa4ce49e3944e4e1a3ee9f79aa2fafe70c0edd2c0d61.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/5f/5fd011917170ef1f098b875060ad48d3ea5d33a1d16d369ae58614c53cd29c9c.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256728 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026f45f2-3480-501a-b813-16b2c86698bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/57f5858f1ccae96a169ee0aac3f106bd44b3de8282412389f4eb8d3ad8b66af9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253685} {:answer/id #uuid "27b268f0-4ffc-54a5-9920-488afedf2f20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c5/c5c0cc02a032916c8f9216673f23f3db81535828b14e51889942ffaf452ac3cb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254023} {:answer/id #uuid "67bd03df-6493-53ce-b107-795c206a4430" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e7/e76b318c2f347bc59746ea0dc88c838ad46694724563867a0ee94318c826d887.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254608} {:answer/id #uuid "88069253-0215-50cc-b496-a51baed841a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/34/346e6bb41b8dd3b353dfedbc55eb25690396d5492d18199ba99a12523452db0a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254878} {:answer/id #uuid "9e64a607-79af-580d-9eca-019fa0b4a565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/08/08a6451e354eaaad23ef233a8c43d16512a2ed33257cd9e9b1257ef5eaaf558b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88069253-0215-50cc-b496-a51baed841a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/34/346e6bb41b8dd3b353dfedbc55eb25690396d5492d18199ba99a12523452db0a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254878} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e428f18-510c-5a07-b305-8e470386def5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255939}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "43fea3a1-3b04-5702-a0ac-bdd3d7956101" :question/math-academy-id "q-122834" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=-1,$ which gives the vertical hyperbola ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=1$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0,$ which gives the pair of lines $y=\\pm x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2,$ which gives the horizontal hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/ce/cecc7f28c20523aac9f153022d64da4233ce995e05df3b3e2886b7fb3b20d089.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move from left to right in the right half-plane $x>0,$ and increases as we move from right-to-left in the left half-plane $x<0.$\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/03/037f46d3fdd190749c2aefe51222475fdd337a7b3ce53e6809048e3491a31a47.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/34/346e6bb41b8dd3b353dfedbc55eb25690396d5492d18199ba99a12523452db0a.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6926dfa-4ca0-5d9b-bb91-d663dc9fd39f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9b/9b8e151985327da40d1fb3044c7f4c785a9f0fcbaa5d71d7f865d4b3fee38ab9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383022} {:answer/id #uuid "2226c493-e827-540e-b916-5dff3335baf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/00/00b722a09fef4428d17fc7ff6a301b3860e798b20c44126b63cd381ad2884398.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383023} {:answer/id #uuid "1faef452-1968-5f42-b62d-394dc63eb8f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/417f9bd99ad7032ffac7ce258c5122d932b1bb1610237ef030f077ca56815bec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383024} {:answer/id #uuid "77afea4d-6f7d-5b97-a1b1-fcdcc7652a4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/04/04f52ccb3eed1f0377edebf34607812f2c8eefe27e910e250959e8fa39ef13d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383025} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f42144-a862-5e50-bcf2-5c21c8fe56a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ae/ae9e6dc29361d667