592186387312}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70739974-680b-512e-bb5a-c7c80411f351" :question/math-academy-id "q-122838" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f7dfad4e44184f68de36d455e99895fce8c879d4b053c06aa5805c423bdb1bfc.png)\n\nThe gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\nObserve that the given field vectors are perpendicular to the level curves in the following picture.\n\n![](images/9f/9f22df07aac0933106381dce4571e674d656428bf7a976e16bed6d46898f814f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/ce/cecc7f28c20523aac9f153022d64da4233ce995e05df3b3e2886b7fb3b20d089.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "758d7c9c-7190-58ba-88ca-6ba4209fb9ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/02/02e2b36b593aef6b8477a6e3a546a7a367cd6c7c8de4642684f418222e0d1c96.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407560} {:answer/id #uuid "b0547ab6-4eb4-5605-bedc-e8c09b74d5ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8f/8f79cfa6b73725479bd76db48b212a60b04ed91f3355c9d135e8cf176b3a6b8f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407561} {:answer/id #uuid "b6af969e-293e-5aa6-8efd-61f10b87dbac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/42/426f761b56bbba7549a5d76ecc6340461c3bc76627c9664a46ef4c6063ee7039.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407562} {:answer/id #uuid "e719fb82-e005-592a-9a1d-c08ce892673d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5f/5f4468465de36289d11b86acb35c38e6d2034ca220d6559ec93b5085d7437d5c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407563} {:answer/id #uuid "eeddcc87-ac3b-50d2-be15-6abdd930a045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5a/5a05d0be8d775cfa97ae507da2ce7904a11b0f7c8df14d51f2e95f17925f64f7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeddcc87-ac3b-50d2-be15-6abdd930a045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5a/5a05d0be8d775cfa97ae507da2ce7904a11b0f7c8df14d51f2e95f17925f64f7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407564} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cef7ae1-6ba6-5fd3-82d2-e1546e8de597" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407565}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8917ffe4-7713-5b06-82b5-c913e5f2e407" :question/math-academy-id "q-122880" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=6x+2y?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get $6x+2y=-1,$ which gives the line $y=-3x-\\frac{1}{2}$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get $6x+2y=0,$ which gives the line $y=-3x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get $6x+2y=2,$ which gives the line $y=-3x+1$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/d6/d6c45af410df1ae974062b777b856b3960e72ad13e14d37a48e5a92cebf74cd1.png)\n\n \n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as both $x$ and  $y$ increase. \n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa601a2bb31bb8c009f554361a79135daf1d7abd2779eaf1ae8b2431a01eafa.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/5a/5a05d0be8d775cfa97ae507da2ce7904a11b0f7c8df14d51f2e95f17925f64f7.png)"}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179714 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179576 :question/id #uuid "2eaebefb-6b18-5689-a746-0d5dc595991c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9453" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = e^{2x-3y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2x-3y}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-3y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2x-3y}) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{2x-3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-3y}\\,i-3{e}^{2x-3y}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd5c5fd8-409c-5b92-a0f1-4466c76cdbe0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b255ed32-00b2-5c02-8fdf-4137e45f1f94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - y)i + (3y - x - 2)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177454} {:answer/id #uuid "07aeabaa-1756-5d7c-b175-364dae9da70e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x - y)i + (6y - x - 4)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177455} {:answer/id #uuid "0ac9617b-4aa8-5c9d-9329-1e2364c1eed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - y)i + (3x - y - 2)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177456} {:answer/id #uuid "56d77004-8bf3-5e3b-9345-4eb6281965f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2y - x)i + (6y - y - 4)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177457} {:answer/id #uuid "5732c3e7-79ac-51b8-b415-739696a1c978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6y - x - 4)i + (2x - y)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177458}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ca579634-59cd-5d79-9ef4-86d15c0bb68b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177453}] :question/id #uuid "9fe868ae-14fa-5f36-b879-4b4d02b56eea" :question/math-academy-id "q-122399" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} - xy + 3y^{2} - 4y$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba475e2-be08-57e4-adec-8abff6266246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i+\\frac{2x}{2y-z}\\,j+\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253761} {:answer/id #uuid "1b833134-f253-5096-a14a-d79dffc93bf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i+\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\,j+\\frac{2x}{z-2y}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253903} {:answer/id #uuid "1d588fd7-e3d7-55a7-9d28-9f3428789f98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i+\\frac{x}{2y-z}\\,j+\\frac{x}{2y-z}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253917} {:answer/id #uuid "69fb1b09-9263-5539-88e5-0d8613d52b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i+\\frac{2x}{2y-z}\\,j+\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254624} {:answer/id #uuid "6e94b4d4-2cb7-572a-9f0d-7088c7794500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i+\\frac{2x}{z-2y}\\,j+\\frac{2x}{z-2y}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ba475e2-be08-57e4-adec-8abff6266246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i+\\frac{2x}{2y-z}\\,j+\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253761} :answer-field/id #uuid "421569dd-cfb3-59c7-8de4-b002df6d520d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256046}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68db203b-e688-5d3a-9ec7-9ce2f2bf5402" :question/math-academy-id "q-122404" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{z-2y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i-\\frac{2x}{z-2y}\\,j+\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256676 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0820aec6-52e0-5e49-a59a-3bbb2eaf3087" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{yz}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{xz}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253728} {:answer/id #uuid "28546720-1acd-59ba-82be-bb4ffaf85ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "yz\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,i+xz\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,j+xy\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254032} {:answer/id #uuid "707b941f-3995-54da-8e24-b82c909648d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254666} {:answer/id #uuid "86a81cb8-c83d-53e2-85a7-df445fd4a517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2yz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2xz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,j+\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254861} {:answer/id #uuid "d766fd6b-8bc8-553e-b31b-478e1a